Release 2 — Core Business Capability
US11 — Validate User Provisioning Eligibility

Story Points: 5

Title: Validate User Eligibility for Dataverse Provisioning

User Story:
As the automated provisioning service, I need to validate whether a detected Entra user is eligible for the target Dataverse environment so that only valid users proceed into the provisioning process.

Business Value:
Prevents provisioning attempts for disabled, invalid, unlicensed, or otherwise ineligible users and reduces avoidable failures.

Scope:
Validate the candidate user against the agreed Entra and Dataverse eligibility rules before attempting any Dataverse user creation or activation.

Technical Requirements:
The service should validate at minimum the Entra user identity, enabled/disabled state, required group membership, environment security-group eligibility if applicable, and any licensing or account criteria required by the client.

Acceptance Criteria:

The Function receives a candidate user from the Release 1 change-detection process.
The candidate user's Entra Object ID is available.
The user record can be resolved in Microsoft Graph.
The user's enabled/disabled state is evaluated.
Required group membership is validated.
Environment security-group eligibility is validated where applicable.
Required licensing or approved eligibility criteria are evaluated.
Eligible users are allowed to continue.
Ineligible users do not proceed into Dataverse provisioning.
The reason for rejection is logged.
Eligibility results include the correlation ID and target user identifier.
No sensitive user information is written unnecessarily to logs.
A failure to evaluate eligibility is distinguishable from an ineligible-user result.

Support Needed If Access Is Missing:
Entra/Identity Admin for user, licensing, and security-group validation. Power Platform Admin for Dataverse eligibility/environment-security-group rules.

Dependencies: US10.

Definition of Done:
An eligible candidate proceeds to Dataverse lookup, while an ineligible test user is safely rejected with a clear logged reason.

US12 — Detect Existing Dataverse User

Story Points: 3

Title: Determine Whether Candidate User Already Exists in Dataverse

User Story:
As the provisioning service, I need to determine whether an eligible Entra user already exists in Dataverse so that the process is idempotent and does not attempt unnecessary duplicate provisioning.

Business Value:
Supports both new-user onboarding and reconciliation of users who already exist in Dataverse.

Scope:
Query the Dataverse System User table using the approved identity attributes and classify the result as existing, disabled, or missing.

Technical Requirements:
The preferred lookup should use the user's Entra Object ID or another approved immutable identity field where possible.

Acceptance Criteria:

The Function successfully authenticates to Dataverse.
The target user is searched using an approved unique identifier.
An existing enabled System User is correctly identified.
An existing disabled System User is distinguishable from an enabled user.
A missing System User is correctly identified.
Duplicate matching records are treated as an exception rather than arbitrarily selected.
Existing users do not trigger duplicate provisioning.
Missing users are routed to the provisioning path.
Existing users can continue into the security/readiness reconciliation path.
Lookup failures are logged with correlation ID and target user identifier.
Dataverse API errors are distinguishable from a legitimate "user not found" result.

Support Needed If Access Is Missing:
Power Platform/D365 Admin if the Application User lacks permission to read systemuser or required identity fields.

Dependencies: US11.

Definition of Done:
The solution reliably identifies an existing, disabled, or missing Dataverse user and routes each case correctly.

US13 — Provision Missing Dataverse User

Story Points: 8

Title: Provision Eligible Missing User into Dataverse

User Story:
As the automated provisioning service, I need to provision an eligible Entra user who does not yet exist in Dataverse so that the user is created before first interactive login.

Business Value:
This is a core business requirement: downstream processes must be able to work with the user before the user signs into Dynamics 365.

Scope:
Implement and validate the client's approved Dataverse on-demand user-provisioning mechanism.

Technical Requirements:
The exact provisioning operation must first be validated in DEV. The Function must use the UAMI-backed Dataverse Application User and approved least-privilege role.

Acceptance Criteria:

An eligible candidate missing from Dataverse reaches the provisioning step.
The approved Dataverse user-provisioning operation is called successfully.
The request uses the managed identity/Application User authentication model.
No client secret is required.
The target Entra identity is used correctly during provisioning.
A System User record is created or synchronized into Dataverse.
The created user can be queried after the provisioning operation.
The created user's Entra identity matches the source user.
Provisioning does not create duplicate System User records.
An already-existing user does not enter the create path.
Provisioning failures are logged with Dataverse error information.
A non-retryable failure is distinguishable from a transient failure.
The operation is safe to retry without creating duplicate users.
Successful provisioning is logged with correlation ID and user identifier.
The implementation is proven with a user who has never logged into the target Dynamics environment.

Support Needed If Access Is Missing:
Power Platform/D365 Admin to validate the supported provisioning API/action, assign required privileges, troubleshoot System User creation, and confirm tenant/environment prerequisites.

Dependencies: US12.

Definition of Done:
A never-before-provisioned eligible test user is successfully created/synchronized into DEV Dataverse without logging into Dynamics first.

US14 — Activate User Security Context Before First Login

Story Points: 8

Title: Activate and Validate Dataverse User Security Context Before First Login

User Story:
As the provisioning service, I need to execute an approved operation in the target user's context so that Dataverse evaluates the user's effective security before interactive login.

Business Value:
Ensures newly provisioned users are operationally usable by downstream Dynamics processes immediately.

Scope:
Use the approved Dataverse impersonation mechanism to execute a user-context validation operation such as WhoAmI.

Technical Requirements:
The Application User must possess the approved impersonation capability. The target user's Entra Object ID should be used through the supported Dataverse impersonation header/mechanism.

Acceptance Criteria:

The target System User exists before impersonation is attempted.
The Dataverse Application User has the approved impersonation privilege.
The Function can execute the approved user-context request.
The request identifies the intended target user.
The returned identity corresponds to the expected target user.
The target user does not need to log into Dynamics first.
A successful activation/validation result is recorded.
An impersonation authorization failure is logged distinctly.
A nonexistent or invalid target user is handled safely.
No elevated personal developer credentials are used at runtime.
The operation is executed through managed identity authentication.
Correlation ID and target user identifier are included in telemetry.

Support Needed If Access Is Missing:
Power Platform/D365 Admin for impersonation privileges and security-role configuration. InfoSec may need to review/approve the impersonation capability.

Dependencies: US13 for new users; existing users from US12 may also enter this path.

Definition of Done:
A provisioned user who has never logged in can successfully be evaluated in their own Dataverse security context.

US15 — Validate or Align Business Unit

Story Points: 5

Title: Validate and Align User Business Unit

User Story:
As the provisioning service, I need to verify that a provisioned user is associated with the correct Dataverse Business Unit so that security and operational ownership behave as designed.

Business Value:
Ensures the user is placed into the correct organizational/security boundary.

Scope:
Read the user's current Business Unit and compare it with the configured target Business Unit. Update only if the approved business rules require it.

Technical Requirements:
Business Unit mapping should remain environment-configurable and should not be hard-coded.

Acceptance Criteria:

The current Business Unit of the target user can be retrieved.
The configured expected Business Unit is resolved.
A user already in the correct Business Unit is left unchanged.
A Business Unit mismatch is detected.
If movement is approved, the user is moved to the target Business Unit.
If movement is not approved, the mismatch is logged for support.
Any Business Unit update is verified after execution.
Errors are logged with correlation ID and user identifier.
The logic is safe for repeated execution.
Business Unit mapping is configurable.

Support Needed If Access Is Missing:
Power Platform/D365 Admin for BU read/update privileges and to confirm the permitted BU movement rules.

Dependencies: US14.

Definition of Done:
The user is either confirmed in the correct Business Unit or safely aligned according to the approved configuration.

US16 — Validate Entra Group Team Access

Story Points: 5

Title: Validate Dataverse Entra Group Team Access

User Story:
As the provisioning service, I need to verify that the user's Entra membership results in the expected Dataverse Group Team access so that team-based security becomes effective before first login.

Business Value:
Confirms that the client's Entra-driven security model is functioning as designed.

Scope:
Identify the configured Dataverse Group Team for the source Entra group and validate the relationship/effective access for the provisioned user.

Technical Requirements:
Mappings between Entra group IDs and Dataverse team IDs should be configuration-driven.

Acceptance Criteria:

The source Entra provisioning group is known.
The mapped Dataverse Group Team can be resolved.
The team is confirmed to be the expected Entra-backed team type.
The team's associated Entra group identity is validated where available.
The provisioned user's source Entra group membership is confirmed.
The user can be evaluated for effective access through the mapped Group Team.
A missing Group Team mapping is logged as a configuration error.
A missing/invalid Dataverse team is logged clearly.
Multiple approved group mappings are supported if required.
Validation does not manually duplicate group-team membership where Dataverse should manage it automatically.
Results are included in the provisioning telemetry.

Support Needed If Access Is Missing:
Power Platform/D365 Admin for Dataverse Group Team configuration and team inspection. Entra/Identity Admin if group membership or group Object IDs cannot be validated.

Dependencies: US14 and US15 where BU alignment affects the security model.

Definition of Done:
The solution confirms that the provisioned user is associated with the expected Entra-driven Dataverse Group Team security path.

US17 — Validate Effective Security Roles

Story Points: 5

Title: Validate Effective Dataverse Security Access

User Story:
As the provisioning service, I need to verify that the user receives the expected security access through configured Dataverse Group Teams so that the user is operationally ready.

Business Value:
Prevents a situation where the System User exists but lacks the security required to perform their job.

Scope:
Validate the expected roles/access associated with the mapped Group Team configuration.

Technical Requirements:
The expected role mappings should be configurable or otherwise documented as environment configuration.

Acceptance Criteria:

Expected security roles for the user's provisioning path are identified.
Roles associated with the mapped Dataverse Group Team can be read.
The expected roles are present on the appropriate Group Team.
Missing expected roles are detected.
Unexpected configuration does not silently pass validation.
The user's resulting effective security can be validated through an approved method.
Security validation does not require assigning direct roles to the user unless explicitly part of the design.
Results are logged with correlation ID and user identifier.
Security-role validation is idempotent.
A security-configuration failure prevents the process from declaring the user fully provisioned.

Support Needed If Access Is Missing:
Power Platform/D365 Admin for security-role inspection, Group Team role assignments, privilege-depth review, or security diagnostics.

Dependencies: US16.

Definition of Done:
The required security roles/access are confirmed effective through the approved Dataverse team-based security model.

US18 — Validate Queue Access

Story Points: 3

Title: Validate Required Dataverse Queue Access

User Story:
As the provisioning service, I need to verify any required queue access for the provisioned user so that the user is ready for operational work routing.

Business Value:
Ensures queue-dependent workloads can use the newly provisioned user immediately.

Scope:
Resolve configured queues and validate required access or membership according to public/private queue behavior.

Technical Requirements:
Queue handling should only be performed where it is part of the approved business requirement.

Acceptance Criteria:

Required queue configuration can be resolved.
Queue type can be identified.
Public queues do not receive unnecessary explicit membership operations.
Private queue membership is validated where applicable.
Missing private-queue membership is added only if approved by the solution design.
Existing queue membership is not duplicated.
Queue changes are verified after execution.
Missing queue configuration is logged clearly.
Queue operation failures are included in the provisioning result.
The user cannot be marked fully ready if mandatory private queue access is missing.
Queue logic is safe for repeat execution.

Support Needed If Access Is Missing:
Power Platform/D365 Admin for queue configuration, private queue membership permissions, and business-rule confirmation.

Dependencies: US14; may also depend on US15–17 depending on the final design.

Definition of Done:
All mandatory queue requirements are confirmed or applied for the provisioned user.

US19 — Validate Record Ownership Readiness

Story Points: 5

Title: Validate Newly Provisioned User as Dataverse Record Owner Before First Login

User Story:
As a downstream business process, I need a newly provisioned user to be available as a valid Dataverse record owner before their first Dynamics login so that ETL, migration, routing, and other automated processes can assign records immediately.

Business Value:
This proves the principal business requirement for the entire solution.

Scope:
Use a controlled DEV test record to verify that a newly provisioned, never-logged-in user can participate in an approved ownership/assignment scenario.

Technical Requirements:
The test must use an agreed business table/record and should not create unintended business data outside the DEV test scope.

Acceptance Criteria:

A test user is added to the approved Entra provisioning group.
The user is detected by the Release 1 delta process.
The user passes eligibility validation.
The user exists or is successfully provisioned into Dataverse.
The user's security context is activated/validated.
Business Unit validation succeeds.
Expected Group Team access is confirmed.
Expected security access is confirmed.
Required queue access is confirmed where applicable.
The user has not interactively logged into Dynamics prior to the test.
An approved DEV record can be assigned to or otherwise associated with the target user as required by the business scenario.
The assignment succeeds without manual user login.
The assignment is visible in Dataverse.
A failed ownership test prevents the user from being marked fully provisioned.
The complete test is traceable using correlation ID and user identifier.
The result is documented as Release 2 acceptance evidence.

Support Needed If Access Is Missing:
Power Platform/D365 Admin for record assignment privileges and troubleshooting. Business Application Owner for selecting the appropriate DEV table/record and confirming the business acceptance test.

Dependencies: US11–US18.

Definition of Done:
A never-before-logged-in test user can be successfully used in the approved Dataverse record-ownership scenario after automated provisioning.
